Date: Wed, 24 Nov 93 09:27:18 EST
From: Tim Freeman <tsf@cs.cmu.edu>
Subject: GP PRNG beats human PRNG?

From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
>The main interests of current GP researchers are complex problems like
>protein classification, robotics, and the like.  But as a little
>side-effort, Koza has already used GP to generate a PRNG 
>with higher entropy than any human-designed algorithm. 

Er, Koza would have to know all human-designed algorithms to be able
to assert this, which seems unlikely.  Also, if Koza is human, then we
have an immediate contradiction.  :-)

There are PRNG's that are cryptographically strong, in the sense that
if there is a polynomial-time algorithm that has a better than 50 +
epsilon% chance of guessing the next bit for some positive epsilon, we
can convert that algorithm to one that breaks various cryptography
problems in polynomial time.  (Definition quoted from memory, hence a
detail or two is probably wrong.)  

I don't know how to measure the entropy of a string of pseudo-random
bits, but it seems that if the output from the CSPRNG is
distinguishable from truly random bits by any polynomial-time
algorithm, then we have a tool for guessing the next bit (simply pick
the bit that makes the sequence look less random in the eyes of the
algorithm that notices the distinction).  If we can measure entropy in
polynomial time, then there seems to be a contradiction.

Tim

