Interesting idea.  But it would seem to require:

1.)  A network of machines which would carry the private "Extropians.*"
hierarchy.  This would be easiest to do in cases where individual
Extropians readers are owners/administrators of the machines which
they receive extropians on.

2.)  That people who are not owners/admins of their own machines to do
their reading/posting online, via an NNTP client, rather than offline,
as many do.  (Alternative: development of an offline NNTP client (if
indeed they don't already exist.))

3.) Adequate security in order to assure that Extropians message
distribution is limited to subscribers.  (Does NNTP allow per-user
authentication?  I'm under the impression that it only allows
per-machine or per-domain authentication...)

4.) Distribution of managment.  The administrator of each machine in the
distribution network would have to cooperate and communicate with the
other admins.

Advantages would be:

1.) Use of subgroups to facilitate division of threads:
"extropians.biz.start-ups", "extropians.tech.nano", etc.

2.) Distribution of processor and bandwidth load.

3.) Filtering in the hands of readers, where it belongs.  Granted, this
capability is already here with the Panix-based command processor, or 
in instances where people use their own mail filters.  But software for
filtering Usenet messages is more widely deployed than software for filtering
mail (at least in my experience) and would thus be more readily available 
to readers.

4.) Potential for "PPL" list filtering: It would be possible to create
a group which would be used for posting and reading of "kill"
messages.  This would be best handled automatically by an augmented
newsreader.  People could choose to use or ignore kill messages posted
by particular users.  Unpopularity of a particular post could be roughly
estimated by the number of kill messages it receives.  Likewise,
"recommend" messages, the inverse of kill messages, could be used to
recommend particular messages to the attention of others.

