derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn) writes:
>Charlie Stross:

>> Here in the UK I did a back-of-envelope calculation a few months ago.
>> There are 30 Million phones here. Assume an average of 1 hour/day
>> usage. Allow 2kbits/sec bandwidth for an uncompressed voice call.

>> That's without compression, of course ...

>Interesting thing to calculate.  I'd be really interested in the
>actual average use (though 1 hour seems approximately reasonable).
>Unfortunately, the 2kbits/sec uncompressed figure is off by at
>least an order of magnitude.  With 8-bit samples (about the minimum
>that could be used), and a 4K sampling rate (not really enough, but
>being generous), that's 32kbits/sec.  

Huh?

I thought the bandwidth of a phone line was about 2 kHz -- part of the
reason why compression protocols tend to be used for rates faster
than 2400 baud. The human voice is mostly concentrated in a range from
200 Hz to 2 kHz, after all. Moreover, it's worth noting that even
CD's, which try to cover the entire audio spectrum, only use 44
kbits/sec (to cover a 20 kHz range). While there's some dispute about
CD's sounding 'flat' on audiophile HiFi systems because they lose the
ultrasonic harmonics, the phone system is undoubtedly a much lower
fidelity (and lower bandwidth) system than a CD player!

>                                         There are ways to compress
>voice so that it's more or less understandable at 5kbits/sec (used
>by the military), but those would require vast (unrealistic) amounts
>of computing power for your telephone company to have installed to do
>the compression.  Realistically, 10kbps is a minimum figure with feasible
>compression.

"Unrealistic" is relative. If it was done in software I might agree,
but a single modern DSP chip capable of handling about 20 MIPS could
do, as far as I can tell, all the compression necessary for >100 (and
possibly as many as 1000) voice channels. _If_ my lower estimate of
sampling rates is accurate, that is.

>Now, even if the 2kpbs figure was right, you dropped another decimal
>point:  30,000 gigabytes (1MB * 30M phones) is right.  1000 digital
>exchanges -> 30 GB / exchange / day == ~10 4MB DAT tapes.  Given
>the actual uncompressed rate of at least 20kbps, now we're talking
>about 100 DAT tapes per day per exchange.  Still possible, but logistically
>rather more difficult; per month, the phone company (or the government,
>or whoever) would be buying three million DAT tapes.  If such is happening
>it shouldn't be very difficult to verify.

Indeed, and I'm a bit red-faced over it.  However, please allow me to
move the goal-posts over to my side of the pitch :-) For a _big_ data
monitoring organization I'd go for something like Digital Paper.  This
stuff was hyped by ICI who developed it a couple of years ago -- then it
went very quiet.  Digital Paper is basically a very cheap WORM medium; a
plastic film that can take write-once optical recording.  The
low-capacity tapes ICI showed could store something like 50Gb, and tapes
the size of a VHS cartridge could handle on the order of 1-2 Tbytes.

Now plugging a tape drive with a digital paper streamer into each main
exchange might generate a tape a day at each main switch, and be
_very_ hard to spot ... and the logistical problem at the archive site
has been pruned down to about 2-300 tapes per year. ("This is our
autochanger. It can load any phone call placed in the UK in the past
six months. Any questions?" "Yeah. Can I listen to one?" "Okay son,
what name would you like to filter for?" "Diana Windsor ...")

The point is, whatever the medium used to record the data (it could
even be as analogue tracks on old 78-rpm records, for all I know), the
storage requirement is within the reach of an organization with a
budget on the order of the hundreds of millions available to NSA or
GCHQ. 


