Date: Wed, 24 Nov 1993 11:20:16 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Comments on NSA

Charlie Stross:

> Huh?
> 
> I thought the bandwidth of a phone line was about 2 kHz -- part of the
> reason why compression protocols tend to be used for rates faster
> than 2400 baud. The human voice is mostly concentrated in a range from
> 200 Hz to 2 kHz, after all. Moreover, it's worth noting that even
> CD's, which try to cover the entire audio spectrum, only use 44
> kbits/sec (to cover a 20 kHz range). While there's some dispute about
> CD's sounding 'flat' on audiophile HiFi systems because they lose the
> ultrasonic harmonics, the phone system is undoubtedly a much lower
> fidelity (and lower bandwidth) system than a CD player!

I sincerely wish that this were true, because it would mean that
voice apps would work much better on PCs than they do today, but
alas, it is not so.  Telephones may have a frequency response of
2khz (though that would be a particularly poor phone line, and
most phones do much better [good thing, or we couldn't understand
each other over the phone!]), but that's not the whole issue.  

It's true that the "primary" frequency of vowel sounds are rather
low, but it's the overtones that are important in distinguishing
an 'aa' from an 'ee' and so on.  These use higher frequencies.
Fire up a sampling app on your PC and try recording your voice
at 2000, 4000, and 8000 samples per second and you'll see what
I mean.  You may be able to make out most of the words at 4000
(because, as you say, many of the frequencies are below 2khz),
but you'll need an 8khz sampling rate to do a truly understandable
job, which is why (for example) the MIME e-mail standard for
transmitting voice uses an 8khz sampling rate -- believe me, they'd
love to use a lower rate if it was feasible.

Now, a CD [mono] does NOT use 44kbits/sec!  It uses a 44khz sampling
rate, but each sample is 16 bits: about 706 kbits/sec.  For low-grade
sound, 8-bit samples are adequate.

> "Unrealistic" is relative. If it was done in software I might agree,
> but a single modern DSP chip capable of handling about 20 MIPS could
> do, as far as I can tell, all the compression necessary for >100 (and
> possibly as many as 1000) voice channels. _If_ my lower estimate of
> sampling rates is accurate, that is.

What kind of compression are you talking about?  The algorithms
used for large-compression voice (9600 or even 4800 bits/sec), based
on sophisticated linear prediction models, will eat that DSP chip
of yours for a single channel.  Simplistic "generic" compression
of sound signals is remarkably hard and you're lucky to get a 3:1
ratio on a typical voice-app 8khz x 8bit sound (yielding 20kbits/sec).
This is why we don't see scads of "lectures on disk" floating around.
A modern fast PC might *just* be able to decompress a 10kbps voice
stream, so that sort of thing is on the edge of becoming mass-marketable.

> The low-capacity tapes ICI showed could store something like 50Gb, and tapes
> the size of a VHS cartridge could handle on the order of 1-2 Tbytes.

Now this is a whole different ballgame.  I did not realize that there
were commercially available terabyte "tape" cartridges.  Could you
tell us more about this?  All of my objections go away if these
are in fact feasible; in fact, LOTS of objections go away in this
case!  Such a tape could hold many hundreds of hours of video!
The complete genomes of thousands of people (actually, many more
with a differential encoding)!  Extropians archives for nearly a month!

> the storage requirement is within the reach of an organization with a
> budget on the order of the hundreds of millions available to NSA or
> GCHQ. 

Well, I maintain that today it's infeasible to get much below 20kbits/sec
per phone line without a dedicated fast DSP per conversation (meaning a
million DSPs or so for all the phone conversations, too expensive today).
Given that, this gives roughly 8,000 terabytes per month.  With a
budget of $8M/month (a hundred million per year, imaginable), this
scenario becomes feasible if and when storage can be gotten for
$1000/terabyte.  If those new devices you mention are in fact in
production (ooh, future shock, I love it!), that would be possible.
For DAT tapes, we're still off by an order of magnitude.

However, I agree that it's only a matter of time before such a thing
is doable, if it isn't today.  I just don't think it's being done
today.

derek

