--from Simon! Levy


I used VMS on my VAX 11/780 from 1980 to 1984. Then I used Symbolics LISP
machines. The last 7 years I've used Macs. Except when using Portal and now
Netcom.

The problem I have with all these wonderful gadgets and systems is
_proliferation of complexity_. Banal complexity at that.

While I'm no Einstein, in more ways than one, I can't imagine Einstein
losing a day of work trying to get his emacs "autowrap" working again when
his site administrator switched from Version 18.xx to Version 19.xx and a
bunch of things broke. (Turned out I wasn't alone...they had "forgotten" to
install some necessary hooks to make everyone's scripts work....and here I
was digging deeper and deeper into my .emacs and .login scripts, tearing my
hair out as I altered scripts, inserted new commands, and was ready to give
up and just use the "Return" key to manually wrap lines! When the folks at
Netcom "remembered" to install the enabling hooks, my old scripts ran
fine.)

And _something_ "breaks" every few months in most Unix systems I know
about. All these wonderful little tools and gizmos that need "Unix gurus"
to fix. 

(Perhaps this is inevitable with most complex systems. I wonder. What I
cherish about my Macs is the "readiness to hand," in Heideggerian terms, of
the interface: I know precisely where files are because I can _see_ them,
and the WYSIWYG interface is consistent in all word processors and
editors...some of the word processor features obviously vary from program
to program, but anyone can edit text with any word processor just by using
the mouse/trackball and the arrow keys--such is clearly not the case in the
world of Unix or VMS or other text-based systems. Not the fault of Unix per
se, but still a source of confusion.)

Ditto for time spent getting Eudora 2.0 to talk to my system, and on and
on. Considering this is just _mail_, I often wonder if it's all worth it.

There was a time when my intellectual energy could be spent almost wholly
on conceptual issues, on reading books. Now, entirely too much time goes
into memorizing epiphenomenol bits of cruft, things like what control key
sequence initiates an auto-reply, what inits on my system bomb other inits.

As Steven Levy put it a MacWorld column a while back, when he was writing
on a typewriter he didn't need to subscribe to "Remington Rand World" and
log onto bulletin boards to find the latest patches that allowed his
telephone to continue working.

(I'll grant you some incredible advantages to computers. Hard to run
"Mathematica" on a typewriter. But I also find myself drowning in a sea of
software that breaks, that needs patches constantly, and that is not
adequately explained anywhere convenient. "Read the man pages" is not what
I want to do when my editor--which I only use a dozen or so commands
in--breaks.)

I'm not arguing for a brain-dead system like "Prodigy," where the user has
few choices in editors, mail readers, etc., just stating my reaction to
tools that force me to spend a day patching an editor, another day
installing new scripts, and on and on. (Part of the confusion doubtless
comes from the fact that I'm cut off from other Unix users, except through
the Net itself. Thus, there's no "Unix guru" down the hallway that can look
at my scripts and .login files and quickly see what's just broken.)

This welter of choices is echoed in the encryption business, where the
couple of dozen major mail systems are all using different interfaces, are
all requiring custom interfaces to PGP, and are all mostly missing built-in
encryption (for this and other reasons). We have in software the "tower of
Babel" problem that so often paralyzes future growth.

How will "the masses" use the current systems if we have to tell them:
"Well, read the man pages and then, if you're using csh, edit your .login
file. Install some Lisp code into emacs that recognizes MIME-compliant Post
Office Protocol sockets. Then it's just a trivial matter of typing "exec
srlpop/strm" and you should be ready to open your mailbox, which, by the
way, is stored in /u21/user/mbox/temp. It's really very simple.And if
you're using ksh..."

I'm not sure my remaining intellectual life should be so oriented toward
minutiae like this.

[DIGRESSION: Business opportunity: This complexity is being attacked by
folks writing simpler editors, recognizing that 99% of users don't need the
power of emacs 19.xx or the latest version of vi...how many people need to
read NetNews from within an emacs buffer while compiling code in another
buffer? I think there's a market for a commercial set of tools that
combines a _stable_ (new versions are always backward-compatible) set of
tools for reading mail and News, and so forth, with a fairly complete
feature set. "Eudora" is successful, but it's only a piece of the solution.
"NewsWatcher" and "Nuntius" are other examples. This could be why "America
Online" (AOL) is doing well. A consistent, Mac-like interface. I assume a
Windows version is coming. If AOL offered a flat-rate service, with local
numbers, like Netcom does, I might switch. A consistent, visual interface
is what I really covet.]

Back to drowning in complexity. This situation is closely related to the
complexity seen elsewhere. I have a cordless phone which interferes with my
answering machine, which is itself frequently "losing it" and having to be
reinitialized. All this complexity, just to use a phone! The canard that
most Americans have their VCRs flashing "12:00" because they're too stupid
to program them is nonsense: after the fourth or fifth power failure, they
realize they're too _smart_ to hunch down and reset the clock every couple
of weeks. Why don't VCRs come with battery back-up of the clock? (Actually,
my new S-VHS deck does. But now that I've had my cable disconnected, what
to record?

Maybe it's just time for me to cut back on the Net. My life is being sucked
into a vortex of triviality.

Please, hold off the suggestions that my problems would be solved if only I
read the man pages on the latest and greatest new editor and switched to
