From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Comments on NSA

Charlie Stross and Derek Zahn have been discussing the logistics of
capturing and storing local phonecalls on DAT tapes (DAT _is_ "tape," I
know). Some comments:

- capturing and storing at the local phone office would be _very obvious_
and very labor-intensive, as either Charlie's or Derek's calculations show.
Too many tapes, too many couriers. 

- under the UK-USA Agreement (cf. Bamford's "The Puzzle Palace"), the USA
can intercept British phone calls, the UK can intercept USA phone calls.
Except in emergencies, in the USA at least the NSA is not supposed to do
this to USA phones (I don't know the situation in Britain, but, lacking a
Constitution of any meaninful sort, I suspect GCHQ does whatever it wants).
Thus, carting tapes back and forth would make this interception fairly
obvious.

- much likelier is interception of the microwave transmissions and even the
cable lines themselves. In the U.S., for example, there is anecdotal
evidence that transcontinental microwave routes were rerouted so as to
cross Indian reservations...this allows interception on a wholesale basis
because Indian reservations are nominally treated as foreign nations and
are exempt in various ways from ordinary laws. (This is a simplification.
Some people believe the CIA used Indian reservations for various operations
banned in the ordinary United States. I don't know the real truth.)

- in other words, direct input into the NSA computers is the likeliest
approach. Using DAT tapes as an intermediary seems too crufty. 

- read Bamford for details on large NSA facilities near satellite downlink
stations in West Virginia, California, England, etc.

(Speculation: A large Comsat downlink/uplink ground station is located at
the end of the Carmel Valley, not far from me. One of the largest
collections of Crays is--surprisingly--located at Fort Ord, also near me.
The Defense Language School, which specializes in foreign language, is also
nearby. And AT&T's "translation service" (real-time two-way translation,
from, say, Swahili to Croatian) is also located there. Finally, the
Monterey Institute for International Studies is believed to be a CIA/DIA
front. Hence, I believe the Monterey Bay area is a hotbed site for
intercepting satellite transmissions, for using the Crays and parallel
computers at the Fort Ord site (nominally a Navy Oceanic Computing Center,
or somesuch), and for employing the human translators when needed to make
the final translations.)

--Tim May (who has a lot of collected stuff on the NSA for his
still-unfinished novel)

